\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Reverse norm one is the Spin carrier in real dimension four}

\p{Let #{V} be a four-dimensional real vector space and let #{Q} be
nondegenerate. Suppress the canonical generator map in Clifford products.
For #{x\in\Cl^{+}(Q)}, the following are equivalent:

##{x\text{ is the Clifford value of an element of }\Spin(Q),
\qquad \operatorname{rev}(x)x=1.}

The forward implication follows from the exact Spin-carrier description
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.range_spinGroup_toUnits}.}

\p{Conversely, suppose #{\operatorname{rev}(x)x=1}. Since the Clifford
algebra is finite-dimensional, the displayed left inverse is also a right
inverse, so #{x} is a unit and #{x^{-1}=\operatorname{rev}(x)}. For
#{v\in V}, put #{w=xvx^{-1}}. The element #{w} is odd, and

##{\operatorname{rev}(w)
=\operatorname{rev}(x^{-1})v\operatorname{rev}(x)
=xvx^{-1}=w.}

In an orthogonal-basis Clifford expansion in dimension four, the odd part has
only degrees one and three. Reversal is #{+1} on degree one and #{-1} on
degree three, so #{w\in V}. Gallier uses this observation in the compact
calculation
\citet{p. 30}{gallier2014clifford}; the same basis-and-reversal argument
applies to every nondegenerate real form in dimension four. Also
#{w^2=Q(v)1}. Conjugation is injective, and its restriction to the
finite-dimensional space #{V} is therefore an automorphism. Thus it defines
#{g\in O(V,Q)}.}

\p{Choose #{y} in the vector-generated Lipschitz group whose twisted action
is #{g}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.lipschitzToOrthogonal_surjective}, and set
#{z=y^{-1}x}. Since #{x} is even, equality of the two actions rearranges for
the grading involution #{\alpha} to

##{\alpha(z)v=vz\qquad(v\in V).}

The graded-centrality theorem therefore gives #{z=c1} for some real #{c}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.exists_eq_algebraMap_of_involute_mul_ι_eq_ι_mul}.
The unit #{z} has #{c\ne0}. Nondegeneracy and positive dimension supply a
nonisotropic vector, so every nonzero scalar unit belongs to the generated
Lipschitz group
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.unitsMap_algebraMap_mem_lipschitzGroup}.
Thus #{x=yz} is Lipschitz. It is even and has reverse norm one, hence lies in
Spin by \ref{fcap-0028}. This dimension-sensitive argument is not asserted in
dimension five or six.}
